  "solution": {
    "approach": "Observe that M XOR (M+1) results in a number consisting of all 1 bits from the least significant bit up to the first 0 in M. Therefore the XOR value N must be of the form 2^k - 1 (all ones in binary). If N is such a number and N != 1 (because M would be 0 which is not positive), then M = (N-1)/2 satisfies the condition. Otherwise no positive M exists.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(1) per test case, O(T) total.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) extra space.",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        int T = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());\n        for (int tc = 0; tc < T; tc++) {\n            long N = Long.parseLong(br.readLine().trim());\n            if (N > 0 && ((N & (N + 1)) == 0) && N != 1) {\n                long M = (N - 1) / 2;\n                out.append(M);\n            } else {\n                out.append(-1);\n            }\n            if (tc + 1 < T) out.append('\\n');\n        }\n        System.out.print(out.toString());\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
